supersonic — source package layout
==================================

This source package uses format 3.0 (quilt) with three upstream tarballs:

  supersonic_<v>.orig.tar.xz
      The upstream repository (git archive of the release tag) with the
      clockwork git submodule archived into clockwork/ at its pinned commit,
      minus the Files-Excluded entries listed in debian/copyright.

  supersonic_<v>.orig-link.tar.xz
      Pristine Ableton Link 4.0 including its asio-standalone submodule
      (the GitHub tag tarball does NOT contain the submodule, so this is
      assembled from a recursive clone). Unpacks to link/. clockwork's four
      Link patches (kept in clockwork/external/*.patch as the single source
      of truth) are applied to it via debian/patches/, path-shifted under
      link/.

  supersonic_<v>.orig-rust-vendor.tar.xz
      `cargo vendor` output for the committed rust/Cargo.lock (the workspace
      whose members depend on clockwork's crates by path, so their registry
      dependencies are vendored too). Unpacks to rust-vendor/; debian/rules
      points cargo at it and builds offline.

All three tarballs, the path-shifted patches, and a snapshot changelog entry
are generated by upstream's scripts/make-debian-source.sh (run by upstream CI
in .github/workflows/debian.yml, which then builds the package in a
network-disconnected container, runs lintian and autopkgtest). A maintainer
can reuse that script or assemble the components by hand as described above.

Build dependencies come from the Debian archive: Catch2, zlib and stb are
the system packages (-DCLOCKWORK_SYSTEM_ZLIB=ON, -DCLOCKWORK_SYSTEM_STB=ON). The audio-device layer is
clockwork's in-tree smoothie (an ISC fork of four JUCE 7 modules); there is
no JUCE build-dependency. Plugin hosting (CLAP/VST3) is compiled out because
the SDKs are fetched at configure time and Debian ships neither. See
docs/DEBIAN-PACKAGING.md in the upstream tree for the full rationale and the
mapping table.
